   "WHAT DUST DOES TO STARLIGHT PART 1  ·  THE STUFF BETWEEN THE STARS Extinction Dust blocks light. Dark clouds look like holes in the Milky Way. Reddening Dust scatters blue light more than red, so stars behind it look redder. Infrared glow Dust is warmed by starlight & glows in infrared. IR telescopes see through it.",
   "PART 1  ·  THE STUFF BETWEEN THE STARS THE 21-CM LINE 21-cm line = radio waves (1420 MHz) from cold hydrogen atoms us Predicted by van de Hulst in 1944; first detected by Ewen & Purcell in 1951. Radio passes through dust, so we can map hydrogen across the whole galaxy. These maps traced the Milky Way's spiral arms. Doppler shifts show how the gas moves: the rotation curve in Deck 18.",
   "OUR NEIGHBORHOOD PART 1  ·  THE STUFF BETWEEN THE STARS The Sun sits inside the Local Bubble: hot, thin gas about 1,000 light-years across. Supernovae blew it out over about the last 14 million years. Its expanding edge pushed gas together: young nearby stars formed on its surface. Right now the Sun is passing through the Local Interstellar Cloud, about 7,500 K.",
